**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 11’s colloids and preceding solution context; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), liquids, interfacial effects, and colloidal/solution discussions; [Analytical Chemistry 2.1 — David Harvey](../sources/Analytical-Chemistry-Harvey.lingua#r_0XMY75E3FRXRGR3BK28V3JAC0T), sampling/preparation issues relevant to a dispersed specimen.

Interfaces contribute chemical and physical behavior that a uniform bulk-phase model can omit. Surface area, adsorption, surface charge, wetting, aggregation, and transport can matter in a fine powder, suspension, emulsion, porous solid, or clean manufactured surface. State the scale and material structure relevant to the observation.

A colloidal dispersion differs from a molecular solution even when both look visually uniform. Its stability and behavior depend on interactions, size distribution, medium composition, and preparation/history. A settled specimen and a well-dispersed specimen can give different sampling and measurement results without changing the total elemental inventory.

Adsorption is associated with an interface; absorption concerns uptake into a bulk region under a specified description. An apparent removal from a solution can involve adsorption, precipitation, filtration, chemical conversion, or several together. A concentration decrease alone does not identify the mechanism or final destination of the component.

**Work:** draw balances for an imagined dissolved component in contact with a solid, distinguishing free solution, surface-associated material, and a separate precipitated phase. List observations needed to distinguish those possibilities. Compare a true solution, suspension, and emulsion using the textbook examples and explain how sampling could bias each result.

Read the entire colloid treatment and use the later equilibrium/kinetic ideas to revisit it. Mineral beneficiation, catalysis, coatings, pharmaceuticals, and semiconductor surfaces will deepen the unique interfacial knowledge. The shared foundation is knowing when a simple bulk concentration or particle diagram is incomplete.

